<p>The above recursive implementation follows the order of preorder travers=
al. Two other common DFS traversal techniques include inorder traversal and=
 postorder traversal. Here=E2=80=99s how they differ:</p>
<div class=3D"table-wrap" style=3D"--table-min-width: 500px;"><table><thead=
><tr><th><strong>Preorder traversal</strong></th><th><strong>Inorder traver=
sal</strong></th><th><strong>Postorder traversal</strong></th></tr></thead>=
<tbody><tr><td><code>process(node)</code></td><td><code>dfs(node.left)</cod=
e></td><td><code>dfs(node.left)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>dfs(node.left=
)</code></td><td><code>process(node)</code></td><td><code>dfs(node.right)</=
code></td></tr><tr><td><code>dfs(node.right)</code></td><td><code>dfs(node.=
right)</code></td><td><code>process(node)</code></td></tr></tbody></table><=
/div>
<p>DFS has many use cases and is the most common choice for tree traversal.=
</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Preorder traversal is the most common type of DFS traversal. It=E2=80=99=
s also used when we need to process the root node of each subtree before it=
s children.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Inorder traversal is used when we want to process the nodes of a tree fr=
om left to right.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Postorder traversal is the least frequently used traversal method, but i=
s important when each node=E2=80=99s subtrees must be processed before thei=
r root.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>The problems in this chapter explore the use cases of DFS in more detail=
, providing practical examples of when and how to apply these traversal met=
hods.</p>
<p><strong>Breadth-first search (BFS)</strong><br>
BFS traverses the nodes of a tree level by level. It processes the nodes at=
 the present level before moving on to nodes at the next depth level.</p>
